    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000021 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000021">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000073"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000488"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The central nervous system agents are medicines that affect the central nervous system (CNS). There are many different types of drugs that work on the CNS, including anesthetics, anticonvulsants, antiemetics, antiparkinson agents, CNS stimulants, muscle relaxants, narcotic analgesics (pain relievers), nonnarcotic analgesics (such as acetaminophen and NSAIDs), and sedatives. Features of CNS drugs are related to their ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier affinity and exhibit CNS activity and can be used in the management of neuro degenerative and psychiatric problems.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000021</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>central nervous system agent</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000021"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>The central nervous system agents are medicines that affect the central nervous system (CNS). There are many different types of drugs that work on the CNS, including anesthetics, anticonvulsants, antiemetics, antiparkinson agents, CNS stimulants, muscle relaxants, narcotic analgesics (pain relievers), nonnarcotic analgesics (such as acetaminophen and NSAIDs), and sedatives. Features of CNS drugs are related to their ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier affinity and exhibit CNS activity and can be used in the management of neuro degenerative and psychiatric problems.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:16489364</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:27439371</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000022 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000022">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000021"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A chemical compound that participates in a chemical reaction that produces dopamine.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000022</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>dopamine precursor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000023 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000023">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000002"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000092"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MI_1140"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_15765"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000056"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000125"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Tyrosine decarboxylase (TDC) is an enzyme with the ability to decarboxylate L-tyrosine into tyramine. Due to the structural similarities between tyrosine and levodopa, TDC found in small intestinal bacteria can convert levodopa to dopamine. Moreover, commonly used decarboxylase inhibitors, carbidopa, benserazide, and methyldopa, do not inhibit TDC. This reduces the efficacy of the drug since the conversion of levodopa to dopamine occurs outside the brain.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000023</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Enterococcus faecalis tyrosine decarboxylase (TDC)</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000023"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Tyrosine decarboxylase (TDC) is an enzyme with the ability to decarboxylate L-tyrosine into tyramine. Due to the structural similarities between tyrosine and levodopa, TDC found in small intestinal bacteria can convert levodopa to dopamine. Moreover, commonly used decarboxylase inhibitors, carbidopa, benserazide, and methyldopa, do not inhibit TDC. This reduces the efficacy of the drug since the conversion of levodopa to dopamine occurs outside the brain.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:25529314</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:30659181</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:31196984</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


