        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000025</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>cardiac therapeutic</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000032 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000032">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000095"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A specific bacterial enzyme that allows E. lenta bacteria to metabolize and deactivate digoxin.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000032</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>cardiac glycoside reductase (cgr) operon</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000032"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A specific bacterial enzyme that allows E. lenta bacteria to metabolize and deactivate digoxin.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:23869020</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000035 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000035">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000095"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MOP_0000615"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000037"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000050"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000032"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>This gene is responsible for encoding proteins which are homologous to bacterial cytochromes, this allows the bacteria to use digoxin as an alternate electron acceptor.  Cgr1 is not directly involved in digoxin reduction, instead, it has a more general function such as electron transfer, or membrane docking. The function of cgr1 is thought to be completing the electron transfer from a membrane-associated electron donor (e.g. the membrane quinone pool) to the [4Fe-4S] cluster of the Cgr2 reductase via its covalently bound heme groups. It is highly up-regulated by digoxin and other cardiac glycosides containing unsaturated butyrolactone rings, such as digitoxin, digoxigenin, and, to a lesser extent, ouabain. It is repressed by arginine but not by ornithine. It is a part of an operon that consists of cgr1 and cgr2.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000035</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Eggerthella lenta cgr1</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000035"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>This gene is responsible for encoding proteins which are homologous to bacterial cytochromes, this allows the bacteria to use digoxin as an alternate electron acceptor.  Cgr1 is not directly involved in digoxin reduction, instead, it has a more general function such as electron transfer, or membrane docking. The function of cgr1 is thought to be completing the electron transfer from a membrane-associated electron donor (e.g. the membrane quinone pool) to the [4Fe-4S] cluster of the Cgr2 reductase via its covalently bound heme groups. It is highly up-regulated by digoxin and other cardiac glycosides containing unsaturated butyrolactone rings, such as digitoxin, digoxigenin, and, to a lesser extent, ouabain. It is repressed by arginine but not by ornithine. It is a part of an operon that consists of cgr1 and cgr2.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:21304654</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:23869020</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000037 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000037">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000095"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MOP_0000569"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_28544"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MOP_0000569"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_42098"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MOP_0000569"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_4551"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000050"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000032"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000056"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000142"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>This gene is involved in the inactivation of the cardiac medication and plant natural product digoxin, thus decreasing drug efficacy and toxicity. It catalyzes the reduction of the alpha,beta-unsaturated butyrolactone ring of digoxin to the inactive metabolite dihydrodigoxin. It likely uses the cytochrome Cgr1 as the physiological electron donor, encoded by the adjacent gene in the locus. cgr2 only reduces digoxin and other cardenolide toxins, such as digitoxin, digoxigenin, ouabain and ouabagenin. Therefore, it is a specialized enzyme present in some gut bacteria E. lenta that protects their human host against ingested plant toxins.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000037</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Eggerthella lenta cgr2</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000037"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>This gene is involved in the inactivation of the cardiac medication and plant natural product digoxin, thus decreasing drug efficacy and toxicity. It catalyzes the reduction of the alpha,beta-unsaturated butyrolactone ring of digoxin to the inactive metabolite dihydrodigoxin. It likely uses the cytochrome Cgr1 as the physiological electron donor, encoded by the adjacent gene in the locus. cgr2 only reduces digoxin and other cardenolide toxins, such as digitoxin, digoxigenin, ouabain and ouabagenin. Therefore, it is a specialized enzyme present in some gut bacteria E. lenta that protects their human host against ingested plant toxins.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:21304654</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:23869020</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:29761785</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000049 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000049">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000493"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Beta-glucuronidases are members of the glycosidase family of enzymes that catalyze breakdown of complex carbohydrates. Bacterial beta-glucuronidase can metabolize to 18-beta-glycyrrhetinic acid (GA) as a main product and to 18-beta-glycyrrhetinic acid-3-O-beta-D-glucuronide (GAMG) as a minor product.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000049</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Beta-glucuronidase</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000049"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Beta-glucuronidases are members of the glycosidase family of enzymes that catalyze breakdown of complex carbohydrates. Bacterial beta-glucuronidase can metabolize to 18-beta-glycyrrhetinic acid (GA) as a main product and to 18-beta-glycyrrhetinic acid-3-O-beta-D-glucuronide (GAMG) as a minor product.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:28578872</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000050 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000050">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000089"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MOP_0001162"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_16450"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MOP_0001162"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_31521"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000056"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000126"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The uridine phosphorylase catalyzes the reversible conversion of uridine to uracil and ribose-1-phosphate in bacteria. This enzyme efficiently deglycosylates capecitabine, deoxyuridine, and doxifluridine.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000050</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Escherichia coli  uridine phosphorylase (udp)</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000050"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>The uridine phosphorylase catalyzes the reversible conversion of uridine to uracil and ribose-1-phosphate in bacteria. This enzyme efficiently deglycosylates capecitabine, deoxyuridine, and doxifluridine.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:32526207</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:34260950</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000068 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000068">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000009"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A pyrimidine deoxyribonucleoside that is a pyrimidine ribonucleoside in which the hydroxy group at the 5&apos; position is replaced by a hydrogen.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000068</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>pyrimidine 5&apos;-deoxyribonucleoside</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000073 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000073">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000008"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A drug class is a set of medications and other compounds that have similar chemical structures, the same mechanism of action, a related mode of action, and/or are used to treat the same disease.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000073</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>drug class</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000074 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000074">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000073"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Products made from botanicals, or plants, that are used to treat diseases or to maintain health are called herbal products, botanical products, or phytomedicines. A product made from plants and used solely for internal use is called an herbal supplement.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000074</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>herbal medication and supplementation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000076 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000076">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000073"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Antineoplastic drugs (ANDs), also known as cytostatic drugs, are a heterogeneous group of chemicals used to control the growth of neoplastic cells. Most ANDs interfere with cell proliferation through DNA destruction, RNA synthesis, or duplication process. Unfortunately, ANDs do not distinguish cancer cells from normal ones, leading to unwanted adverse effects in patients receiving chemotherapy. The International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC) classifies some ANDs as human carcinogens.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000076</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>anti-neoplastic agent</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000076"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Antineoplastic drugs (ANDs), also known as cytostatic drugs, are a heterogeneous group of chemicals used to control the growth of neoplastic cells. Most ANDs interfere with cell proliferation through DNA destruction, RNA synthesis, or duplication process. Unfortunately, ANDs do not distinguish cancer cells from normal ones, leading to unwanted adverse effects in patients receiving chemotherapy. The International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC) classifies some ANDs as human carcinogens.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:27110842</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000077 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000077">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000076"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Topoisomerase inhibitors are chemical compounds that block the action of topoisomerases. Topoisomerase plays key roles in cellular reproduction and DNA organization, as they facilitate the cleavage of single and double stranded DNA to relax supercoils, untangle catenanes, and condense chromosomes in eukaryotic cells. Topoisomerase inhibitors affect these essential cellular processes by preventing topoisomerases from performing DNA strand breaks or prevent the re-ligation step of the topoisomerase mechanism by interacting with topoisomerase-DNA complexes. These topoisomerase-DNA-inhibitor complexes are cytotoxic agents because the un-repaired single and double stranded DNA breaks that they cause can lead to apoptosis and cell death. Due to this ability to induce apoptosis, topoisomerase inhibitors have gained interest as therapeutics against infectious and cancerous cells.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000077</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>topoisomerase inhibitor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000077"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Topoisomerase inhibitors are chemical compounds that block the action of topoisomerases. Topoisomerase plays key roles in cellular reproduction and DNA organization, as they facilitate the cleavage of single and double stranded DNA to relax supercoils, untangle catenanes, and condense chromosomes in eukaryotic cells. Topoisomerase inhibitors affect these essential cellular processes by preventing topoisomerases from performing DNA strand breaks or prevent the re-ligation step of the topoisomerase mechanism by interacting with topoisomerase-DNA complexes. These topoisomerase-DNA-inhibitor complexes are cytotoxic agents because the un-repaired single and double stranded DNA breaks that they cause can lead to apoptosis and cell death. Due to this ability to induce apoptosis, topoisomerase inhibitors have gained interest as therapeutics against infectious and cancerous cells.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:22108601</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:29363591</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000078 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000078">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000077"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Camptothecin (CPT) is a topoisomerase inhibitor. It was discovered in 1966 during a systematic screening of natural products for anticancer drugs. It was isolated from the bark and stem of Camptotheca acuminata (Camptotheca, Happy tree), a tree native to China used in traditional Chinese medicine. CPT has showed anticancer activity in clinical trials, especially against breast, ovarian, colon, lung, and stomach cancers. To maximize the therapeutic effects, four CPT analogues have been approved and are used as antineoplastic agents.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000078</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Camptothecin Analogues</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000078"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Camptothecin (CPT) is a topoisomerase inhibitor. It was discovered in 1966 during a systematic screening of natural products for anticancer drugs. It was isolated from the bark and stem of Camptotheca acuminata (Camptotheca, Happy tree), a tree native to China used in traditional Chinese medicine. CPT has showed anticancer activity in clinical trials, especially against breast, ovarian, colon, lung, and stomach cancers. To maximize the therapeutic effects, four CPT analogues have been approved and are used as antineoplastic agents.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:21051639</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000081 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000081">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000002"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000078"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A member of the class of pyranoindolizinoquinolines that is the carbamate ester obtained by formal condensation of the carboxy group of [1,4&apos;-bipiperidine]-1&apos;-carboxylic acid with the phenolic hydroxy group of (4S)-4,11-diethyl-4,9-dihydroxy-1H-pyrano[3&apos;,4&apos;:6,7]indolizino[1,2- hydrochloride]quinoline-3,14-dione. Used (in the form of its hydrochloride salt trihydrate) in combination with fluorouracil and leucovorin, for the treatment of patients with metastatic adenocarcinoma of the pancreas after disease progression following gemcitabine-based therapy. It is converted via hydrolysis of the carbamate linkage to its active metabolite, SN-38, which is ~1000 times more active.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000081</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>irinotecan</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000089 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000089">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000002"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000090"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000056"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000016"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>These are enzymes responsible for the breakdown and utilization of nucleosides.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000089</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>fluoropyrimidines deglycosylation enzyme</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000090 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000090">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000012"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Thymidine phosphorylase (TP) enzyme catalyzes the phosphorolysis of the nucleosides thymidine (dThd) and deoxyuridine (dUrd) to the corresponding bases thymine and uracil, and deoxyribose-1-phosphate. Thymidine phosphorylase (TP), is also related to Pyrimidine nucleoside phosphorylase (PYNP), which catalyzes the reversible phosphorolysis of pyrimidines in the nucleotide synthesis salvage pathway. In lower organisms (e.g. Bacillus stearothermophilus) PYNP accepts both thymidine and uridine, whereas in mammalian and other higher organisms it is specific for thymidine (designated thymidine phosphorylase, TP). PYNP shares 40% sequence similarity (and presumably significant structural similarity) with human TP.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000090</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>thymidine pyrimidine-nucleoside phosphorylase family</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000090"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Thymidine phosphorylase (TP) enzyme catalyzes the phosphorolysis of the nucleosides thymidine (dThd) and deoxyuridine (dUrd) to the corresponding bases thymine and uracil, and deoxyribose-1-phosphate. Thymidine phosphorylase (TP), is also related to Pyrimidine nucleoside phosphorylase (PYNP), which catalyzes the reversible phosphorolysis of pyrimidines in the nucleotide synthesis salvage pathway. In lower organisms (e.g. Bacillus stearothermophilus) PYNP accepts both thymidine and uridine, whereas in mammalian and other higher organisms it is specific for thymidine (designated thymidine phosphorylase, TP). PYNP shares 40% sequence similarity (and presumably significant structural similarity) with human TP.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:27004974</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:9817849</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000091 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000091">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000002"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000078"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Trastuzumab deruxtecan is a monoclonal antibody medication used for the treatment of breast cancer or for the treatment of gastric or gastroesophageal adenocarcinoma. It is an antibody-drug conjugate composed of an anti-HER2 (human epidermal growth factor receptor 2) antibody, a cleavable tetrapeptide-based linker, and a cytotoxic topoisomerase I inhibitor.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000091</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Trastuzumab deruxtecan</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000092 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000092">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000094"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An enzyme that participates in a chemical reaction that removes a carboxyl group, decarboxylation, from a compound.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000092</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>carboxyl-lyase</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000094 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000094">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000012"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000896"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Lyases are enzymes that catalyze the bond construction and breaking by means other than hydrolysis or redox reactions. Such enzymes are particular in the sense that they only require one substrate for the reaction in one direction, but two substrates for the reverse reaction.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000094</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>lyases family</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000094"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Lyases are enzymes that catalyze the bond construction and breaking by means other than hydrolysis or redox reactions. Such enzymes are particular in the sense that they only require one substrate for the reaction in one direction, but two substrates for the reverse reaction.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:21417217</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000095 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000095">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000002"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000097"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000056"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000098"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>These proteins are part of cardiac glycoside reductase (CGR) pathway. The CGR is responsible for reductive metabolism of cardiac glycoside. The expression of these enzymes can be inhibited by dietary arginine.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000095</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Cytochrome c-type protein</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000095"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>These proteins are part of cardiac glycoside reductase (CGR) pathway. The CGR is responsible for reductive metabolism of cardiac glycoside. The expression of these enzymes can be inhibited by dietary arginine.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:24637603</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000097 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000097">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000012"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Cytochrome (CYP) enzymes catalyze the metabolic reactions of endogenous and exogenous compounds. The superfamily of enzymes is found across many organisms, regardless of type, except for plants. Differences in cytochrome subunits are expected to be critical in deciding the unique substrates that allow this functional diversity.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000097</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>cytochrome protein family</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000098 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000098">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000007"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The process of breaking down of a cardiac therapy drug which improve the heart&apos;s energy production and efficiency.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000098</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>cardiac therapy metabolism</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000099 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000099">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000002"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A monocarboxylic acid that is 1-benzylidene-1H-indene which is substituted at positions 2, 3, and 5 by methyl, carboxymethyl, and fluorine respectively, and in which the phenyl group of the benzylidene moiety is substituted at the para position by a methylsulfinyl group. It is a prodrug for the corresponding sulfide, a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug, used particularly in the treatment of acute and chronic inflammatory conditions. Sulindac is used to relieve pain, tenderness, swelling, and stiffness caused by osteoarthritis (arthritis caused by a breakdown of the lining of the joints), rheumatoid arthritis (arthritis caused by swelling of the lining of the joints), and ankylosing spondylitis (arthritis that mainly affects the spine).</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000099</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>sulindac</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000108 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000108">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000002"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A member of the class of 1-benzothiophenes that is 1-benzothiophene in which the hydrogens at positions 2, 3, and 6 have been replaced by p-hydroxyphenyl, p-[2-(piperidin-1-yl)ethoxy]benzoyl, and hydroxy groups, respectively. Raloxifene is a medication used to prevent and treat osteoporosis in postmenopausal women and those on glucocorticoids. It is also used to reduce the risk of breast cancer in those at high risk. Raloxifene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) and therefore a mixed agonist–antagonist of the estrogen receptor (ER). It has estrogenic effects in bone and antiestrogenic effects in the breasts and uterus.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000108</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>raloxifene</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000125 -->

