        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000844</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>methylation of a molecule</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000845 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000845">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000073"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Analgesics are the drugs which bring insensibility to pain without loosing consciousness. Treatment strategy is generally based on the type of pain. Most of the analgesics are associated with serious side effects, such as NSAIDS can cause severe GI disturbance and opioids can cause addiction.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000845</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>analgesic</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000845"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Analgesics are the drugs which bring insensibility to pain without loosing consciousness. Treatment strategy is generally based on the type of pain. Most of the analgesics are associated with serious side effects, such as NSAIDS can cause severe GI disturbance and opioids can cause addiction.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:27142107</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000846 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000846">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000012"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Pyridoxal 5′-phosphate (PLP) is a cofactor for about 4% of all biochemical reactions. PLP and five other ‘vitamers’, are collectively known as vitamin B6. In general, there are two independent de novo pathways that generate PLP: the deoxyxylulose 5′-phosphate (DXP)-dependent pathway, which is present in most eubacteria (e.g. Escherichia coli), and the ‘DXP-independent’ route, which is widespread (e.g. some eubacteria, Archaea, fungi and plants) and consists of a single enzyme complex, PLP synthase. In the PLP salvage pathway, however, different vitamers are interconverted using various kinase, phosphatase and pyridoxine/pyridoxamine 5′-phosphate oxidase (PNPOx) enzymes. The reaction catalysed by members of the PNPOx family (so-called PdxH proteins) is equivalent to the last step of the DXP-dependent PLP biosynthesis pathway, oxidizing either PNP or PMP to produce PLP. Members of the family utilize flavin mononucleotide (FMN) as a prosthetic group, oxidizing the C4′ alcohol (PNP) or amine (PMP) group to an aldehyde (PLP). During catalysis, FMN is used as the immediate electron acceptor and molecular oxygen as the final electron acceptor, forming hydrogen peroxide under aerobic conditions. Pyridoxal 5′-phosphate biosynthesis is essential for the survival and virulence of Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb), the organism that causes tuberculosis (TB) and is, for this reason, an important drug target at a time when resistance is rising and new drugs are urgently needed.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000846</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Pyridoxamine phosphate oxidase family</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000846"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Pyridoxal 5′-phosphate (PLP) is a cofactor for about 4% of all biochemical reactions. PLP and five other ‘vitamers’, are collectively known as vitamin B6. In general, there are two independent de novo pathways that generate PLP: the deoxyxylulose 5′-phosphate (DXP)-dependent pathway, which is present in most eubacteria (e.g. Escherichia coli), and the ‘DXP-independent’ route, which is widespread (e.g. some eubacteria, Archaea, fungi and plants) and consists of a single enzyme complex, PLP synthase. In the PLP salvage pathway, however, different vitamers are interconverted using various kinase, phosphatase and pyridoxine/pyridoxamine 5′-phosphate oxidase (PNPOx) enzymes. The reaction catalysed by members of the PNPOx family (so-called PdxH proteins) is equivalent to the last step of the DXP-dependent PLP biosynthesis pathway, oxidizing either PNP or PMP to produce PLP. Members of the family utilize flavin mononucleotide (FMN) as a prosthetic group, oxidizing the C4′ alcohol (PNP) or amine (PMP) group to an aldehyde (PLP). During catalysis, FMN is used as the immediate electron acceptor and molecular oxygen as the final electron acceptor, forming hydrogen peroxide under aerobic conditions. Pyridoxal 5′-phosphate biosynthesis is essential for the survival and virulence of Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb), the organism that causes tuberculosis (TB) and is, for this reason, an important drug target at a time when resistance is rising and new drugs are urgently needed.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:26823273</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000847 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000847">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000015"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A chemical process that involves the removal of one or mole electrons from a molecular entity.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000847</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>oxidation of a molecule</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000848 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000848">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000073"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Catechol O-methyltransferase catalyzes the methylation reaction whereby a methyl group from the cofactor S-adenosyl-L-methionine (SAM) is transferred to one of the catecholic hydroxyls. Membrane-bound form COMT (MB-COMT) may be more relevant in inactivation of catecholaminergic neurotransmitters, whereas the soluble form (S-COMT) plays a more important role in inactivation of endogenous and xenobiotic catechols in other tissues. COMT is the primary enzyme that inactivates the catechol neurotransmitter dopamine and the drug L-dopa. L-dopa is used in the clinical treatment of central nervous system (CNS) disorders such as Parkinson&apos;s disease and possibly others (e.g. schizophrenia and depression). Its efficacy is associated with the level of dopamine converted from the drug. Studies have shown that inhibition of COMT activity results in marked reduction of the body clearance of L-dopa and dopamine, leading to a sustained level of dopamine in the brain and improved efficacy. The important role of COMT in treatment of Parkinson&apos;s disease has promoted an area wherein the aims are to design potent and selective COMT inhibitors. Several of these inhibitors have been used as adjuncts to L-dopa therapy.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000848</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) inhibitor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000848"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Catechol O-methyltransferase catalyzes the methylation reaction whereby a methyl group from the cofactor S-adenosyl-L-methionine (SAM) is transferred to one of the catecholic hydroxyls. Membrane-bound form COMT (MB-COMT) may be more relevant in inactivation of catecholaminergic neurotransmitters, whereas the soluble form (S-COMT) plays a more important role in inactivation of endogenous and xenobiotic catechols in other tissues. COMT is the primary enzyme that inactivates the catechol neurotransmitter dopamine and the drug L-dopa. L-dopa is used in the clinical treatment of central nervous system (CNS) disorders such as Parkinson&apos;s disease and possibly others (e.g. schizophrenia and depression). Its efficacy is associated with the level of dopamine converted from the drug. Studies have shown that inhibition of COMT activity results in marked reduction of the body clearance of L-dopa and dopamine, leading to a sustained level of dopamine in the brain and improved efficacy. The important role of COMT in treatment of Parkinson&apos;s disease has promoted an area wherein the aims are to design potent and selective COMT inhibitors. Several of these inhibitors have been used as adjuncts to L-dopa therapy.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:23713800</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000881 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000881">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000073"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Histamine is primarily found in mast cells and basophils, where it is stored in cytoplasmic granules and released along with other inflammatory mediators upon activation in response to diverse immune and non-immune stimuli, including viruses and other pathogens. Histamine exerts multiple (patho)physiological actions by activating four known types of histamine receptors that are designated as H1–H4 and have been associated with allergic inflammation, stimulation of gastric acid secretion, neurotransmission and immune responses, respectively. This led to drugs, such as H1 antihistamines for the management of allergies, H2 receptor antagonists for the treatment of gastrointestinal disorders, a first-in-class H3 receptor antagonist for treating narcolepsy, as well as H4 receptor-targeting compounds that are being evaluated in clinical trials for their potential exploitation in managing inflammatory disorders.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000881</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>histamine (H2-receptor) antagonist</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000881"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Histamine is primarily found in mast cells and basophils, where it is stored in cytoplasmic granules and released along with other inflammatory mediators upon activation in response to diverse immune and non-immune stimuli, including viruses and other pathogens. Histamine exerts multiple (patho)physiological actions by activating four known types of histamine receptors that are designated as H1–H4 and have been associated with allergic inflammation, stimulation of gastric acid secretion, neurotransmission and immune responses, respectively. This led to drugs, such as H1 antihistamines for the management of allergies, H2 receptor antagonists for the treatment of gastrointestinal disorders, a first-in-class H3 receptor antagonist for treating narcolepsy, as well as H4 receptor-targeting compounds that are being evaluated in clinical trials for their potential exploitation in managing inflammatory disorders.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:33206207</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000887 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000887">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000012"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>One particular transcription factors (TF), the fumarate and nitrate reductase regulatory protein (FNR), is among the most widely studied global regulators, activating and repressing transcription of a wide variety of functional genes. FNR is widely conserved across bacteria and is often required for the shift between aerobic and anaerobic metabolism. FNR belongs to the catabolite repressor protein (CRP; also known as cAMP receptor protein)/FNR family of TFs, which plays an important role across bacteria and generally shares a conserved DNA binding site. Like FNR, CRP is well studied and widely conserved across bacteria and responds to variations in glucose availability via changes in the concentration of cyclic AMP. Besides CRP and FNR, the CRP/FNR family of proteins contains other well-studied TFs that respond to a variety of environmental stimuli.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000887</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Crp/Fnr family transcriptional regulator</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000887"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>One particular transcription factors (TF), the fumarate and nitrate reductase regulatory protein (FNR), is among the most widely studied global regulators, activating and repressing transcription of a wide variety of functional genes. FNR is widely conserved across bacteria and is often required for the shift between aerobic and anaerobic metabolism. FNR belongs to the catabolite repressor protein (CRP; also known as cAMP receptor protein)/FNR family of TFs, which plays an important role across bacteria and generally shares a conserved DNA binding site. Like FNR, CRP is well studied and widely conserved across bacteria and responds to variations in glucose availability via changes in the concentration of cyclic AMP. Besides CRP and FNR, the CRP/FNR family of proteins contains other well-studied TFs that respond to a variety of environmental stimuli.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:34636676</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


