        <rdfs:label>Drug self-resistant enzyme</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000902 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000902">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000002"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000830"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MOP_0000619"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_2376"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000056"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000491"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The apg is an acarbose-preferred glucosidase that was discovered in intestinal bacteria from patients with Type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) chronic metabolic disease. The Apg hydrolyses acarbose into small molecules (acarviosine-glucose and acarviosine) with loss of inhibitor function to porcine pancreatic amylase and is widely distributed in human intestinal microorganisms, especially in Klebsiella.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000902</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>apg</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000902"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>The apg is an acarbose-preferred glucosidase that was discovered in intestinal bacteria from patients with Type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) chronic metabolic disease. The Apg hydrolyses acarbose into small molecules (acarviosine-glucose and acarviosine) with loss of inhibitor function to porcine pancreatic amylase and is widely distributed in human intestinal microorganisms, especially in Klebsiella.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:37157031</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000907 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000907">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000049"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>These enzymes perform reactivation of endobiotic glucuronides of small molecules xenobiotics (e.g., cancer drugs, NASAIDSs and consumer products toxins). Examples includes C. perfringens GUS, E. coli GUS, S. agalactiae GUS, and E. eligens GUS.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000907</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Loop 1</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000907"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>These enzymes perform reactivation of endobiotic glucuronides of small molecules xenobiotics (e.g., cancer drugs, NASAIDSs and consumer products toxins). Examples includes C. perfringens GUS, E. coli GUS, S. agalactiae GUS, and E. eligens GUS.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:28578872</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:38754417</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000909 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000909">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000049"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>These enzymes perform reactivation of endobiotic glucuronides of large polysaccharides (e.g., heparan and chondroitin sulfates). Examples includes  Bacteroides uniformis GUS.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <dcterms:contributor rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9259-5280"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>human_microbiome_drug_metabolism_ontology</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>HMDM:0000909</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Loop 2</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HMDM_0000909"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
